text: lay out a long text at its own width on one line on macOS

On macOS, a single-line text that gets exactly its measured width can
still end in "…" (and a wrapping one drop its last word to a second line).
CTTypesetterSuggestLineBreak forgives a width 0.0002 DIPs short of the
line's at most. A float32 sum of a long line's float32 advances loses
more (2e-4 at 570 DIPs), so long lines broke early: line() now sums in
float64, and coreText.shape breaks at width * (1 + 1e-6).

Until MyGo 0.5.0 the patch also folded the room Core Text leaves between
runs of two fonts (a bracket next to a letter of another script, as in
"M4B（含章节）") into the advances; 0.5.0's penAdvances does that upstream
(TestCoreTextLanguageSpacing), so that part is gone. On macOS 26.7 with
0.5.5 alone, TestOwnWidthFits still fails for the long lines (10 cases,
"Downloads start when you finish, and go on in the background." at 20/600
among them) and passes with this patch.

TestOwnWidthFits lays out each text at its own width and checks it keeps
to one line and the glyphs stay within the width.
---
diff --git a/internal/text/coretext_darwin.go b/internal/text/coretext_darwin.go
index a37d0a0..0b0b723 100644
--- a/internal/text/coretext_darwin.go
+++ b/internal/text/coretext_darwin.go
@@ -981,12 +981,16 @@ func (e *coreText) shape(text []rune, style Style, spans []Span, width float32,
 		e.breaks = lineBreaks(&e.seg, e.breaks, text)
 		breaks = e.breaks
 	}
+	// Widths are float32, from Core Text's doubles rounded glyph by glyph:
+	// a text laid out again at the width it measured may come out short
+	// of Core Text's own by more than the 0.0002 DIPs it forgives.
+	breakWidth := float64(width) * (1 + 1e-6)
 	var lines []shapedLine
 	mark := len(e.shapeScratch.lines)
 	for start := 0; start < n; {
 		count := n - start
 		if width > 0 {
-			count = max(ct.suggestLineBreak(typesetter, start, float64(width)), 1)
+			count = max(ct.suggestLineBreak(typesetter, start, breakWidth), 1)
 			// Core Text breaks a word that does not fit a line; carry
 			// on to the next break instead.
 			if breaks != nil {
